        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The overall goal of this study was to characterize novel molecular mechanisms involved in radiation carcinogenesis. This study concentrated on the role played by altered cell proliferation, aberrant mitoses, and aneuploidy/polyploidy cell formation and tested the hypothesis that cells undergoing mitotic catastrophe (MC – these cells are large multinucleated, polyploid cells) after exposure to charged particle radiation are able to escape death, to undergo depolyploidization, to give rise to cell progenies that regain a degree of genomic stability sufficient for them to survive, to proliferate, and to acquire transformation competence thus becoming a focal spot for cancer initiation.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Investigators aimed to determine the mechanisms that favor genomic reorganization in the irradiated cells and also investigated genetic and epigenetic changes that may play a role in the carcinogenesis process. In this contest, they demonstrated: 1) activation of both meiosis-specific genes and specific markers of cancer stem cells in the irradiated cells. These findings point towards the novel notion that unusual pathways of cell division and proliferation are involved in genomic reorganization in irradiated cells that escape death; 2) disruption of normal arrangement of loci in chromosomes that have signatures for different types of cancers. These aberrations are found in both the original irradiated cells and their progeny highlighting the fact that they propagate to subsequent generations and may hinder the overall genetic stability of the cellular system that could then become more prone to mutation and transformation; 3) dramatic decrease in Lsh mRNA and protein levels associated with persistent gamma-H2AX foci (the lymphoid-specific helicase (Lsh) is a gene that is preferentially associated with pericentric heterochromatin and whose suppression leads to CpG demethylation and disruption of proper DNA conformation; these are important functions in our system because changes in cell ploidy, aberrant chromatin organization, aberrant mitoses, and skewed cell proliferation are associated with disruption of normal organization of pericentric heterochromatine. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Findings outline the innovative concept that normal human cells exposed to charged particles mimicking space radiation become polyploid, a tumor hallmark, are minimally affected by cell death, and a percentage of them undergo successful depolyploidization and form progenies. The polyploidization/depolyploidization (reduction division) events are accompanied by morphologic, phenotypic, genetic, and epigenetic changes in which the cells’ DNA content greatly varies, the cells’ chromatin condensation status is greatly altered, proteins involved in DNA repair/remodeling and meiosis-specific proteins involved in pairing of homologous chromosomes are found highly expressed, and proteins involved in the proper establishment and maintenance of DNA methylation at pericentric heterochromatin are greatly depressed. The latter has been shown to lead to disruption of normal organization of chromatin, which in turn is associated with changes in cell ploidy, aberrant chromatin organization, aberrant mitoses, and skewed cell proliferation; all events that occur in our irradiated cells. 
<p>
Furthermore, results provide a mechanistic link between radiation-induced changes in DNA methylation and altered cell proliferation, aberrant mitoses, chromatin condensation statuses, and changes in ploidy in normal human irradiated cells, and support the hypothesis that the totality of these modifications are events linked to genetic and epigenetic modification that might contribute to the overall cellular genetic instability (without greatly affecting cell death) of the exposed populations and their progeny and, thus, in turn contribute to augmented cancer risk from space radiation.
<p>
No datasets exist for this study. A final report was archived.
<p>
